## Self-help first

For most people with primary Raynaud's, the first treatment is **avoiding triggers** and **keeping warm**. Read our tips on [living well with Raynaud's](/conditions/heart-and-circulation/raynauds/living-well/).

If you **smoke**, stopping is one of the best things you can do, because smoking narrows the blood vessels. Your local stop smoking service can help for free.

## Medicines

If self-help is not enough, a GP may offer a medicine that **widens the blood vessels**.

- **Nifedipine** is the medicine most often used. It is a type of medicine called a **calcium channel blocker**. Some people take it every day, and others only in the colder months.
- Other calcium channel blockers, such as [amlodipine](/medicines/amlodipine/) or [diltiazem](/medicines/diltiazem/), may be tried instead.
- If these do not help, a specialist may suggest other medicines, such as [sildenafil](/medicines/sildenafil/).

Common side effects of calcium channel blockers include **headaches**, **flushing** and **swollen ankles**. These often get better after the first few weeks. Tell your GP if they do not.

## Secondary Raynaud's

If your Raynaud's is caused by another condition, treating that condition is important. You may be referred to a specialist, such as a **rheumatologist**.

People with severe secondary Raynaud's may sometimes need treatment in hospital, such as medicine given through a drip, especially if they have **finger ulcers**. Surgery is rarely needed.

## If your job is the cause

If your Raynaud's is caused by **vibrating tools**, tell your employer and your GP. Reducing your exposure to vibration is the most important step. Read about [Raynaud's, work and benefits](/conditions/heart-and-circulation/raynauds/benefits-and-work/).
